WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.048
It's 1:47 on a Thursday morning,

00:00:02.048 --> 00:00:03.800
and we're in a maintenance window.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.342
The change is three lines on LEAF-2.

00:00:06.342 --> 00:00:08.542
It went through peer review this afternoon,

00:00:08.542 --> 00:00:10.640
and the reviewer wrote, looks good to me.

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:13.047
So we push it.

00:00:13.837 --> 00:00:16.237
It puts the rest of LEAF-2 into area two,

00:00:16.237 --> 00:00:17.758
next to its server subnet,

00:00:17.758 --> 00:00:19.397
so the config reads cleaner.

00:00:19.397 --> 00:00:20.627
About a minute later,

00:00:20.627 --> 00:00:22.617
LEAF-2 has dropped off the fabric.

00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:25.553
Let's rewind that.

00:00:26.753 --> 00:00:28.663
Same Thursday, same three lines.

00:00:28.663 --> 00:00:30.513
This time we rehearse it first.

00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:34.801
Nothing gets installed on the network for this.

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:36.901
No agents, no lab of virtual routers,

00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:38.433
and no login on any device.

00:00:39.183 --> 00:00:42.350
Hammerhead reads the configs from before and after the change,

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.087
runs the routing protocols itself,

00:00:44.087 --> 00:00:46.283
and compares the forwarding that comes out.

00:00:47.083 --> 00:00:49.772
Fifteen routes change, on all four devices,

00:00:49.772 --> 00:00:51.523
from an edit to one of them.

00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:54.185
So, is it safe to push?

00:00:54.185 --> 00:00:56.253
That's the question preflight answers.

00:00:57.003 --> 00:00:58.843
It probes every pair of devices,

00:00:58.843 --> 00:01:00.453
before and after the change.

00:01:00.453 --> 00:01:03.098
Six flows that worked before don't work after,

00:01:03.098 --> 00:01:05.283
and every one of them involves LEAF-2.

00:01:06.183 --> 00:01:07.574
So the verdict is fail,

00:01:07.574 --> 00:01:09.993
and the exit code is two. In a pipeline,

00:01:09.993 --> 00:01:11.323
that's where it stops.

00:01:12.223 --> 00:01:15.483
And the cause isn't in the three lines anyone reviewed.

00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:18.031
The other end of each uplink is on a spine,

00:01:18.031 --> 00:01:19.157
still in area zero,

00:01:19.157 --> 00:01:21.823
and OSPF won't form an adjacency across that.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.100
A fair question is why you'd trust the model.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.981
So we check it against real routers.

00:01:28.981 --> 00:01:30.426
Ten of them, running FRR,

00:01:30.426 --> 00:01:33.200
in a lab that's rebuilt and run again each week.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.096
Every route FRR has, the model has too,

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:39.240
and it doesn't make any up.

00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:44.152
Being accurate is half of it.

00:01:44.152 --> 00:01:47.306
The other half is catching the kind of change that actually hurts,

00:01:47.306 --> 00:01:48.740
so we test it on real outages.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.747
There are more than a hundred in the repo.

00:01:51.497 --> 00:01:52.163
In July 2022,

00:01:52.163 --> 00:01:54.928
a maintenance change at Rogers removed a route filter.

00:01:54.928 --> 00:01:57.233
The full routing table flooded into the core,

00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:59.897
and more than twelve million customers lost service.

00:02:00.747 --> 00:02:02.958
We rebuilt that change from the public record,

00:02:02.958 --> 00:02:03.487
and ran it.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.923
The awkward part of that change is that no flow is lost.

00:02:07.923 --> 00:02:09.165
Every path still works,

00:02:09.165 --> 00:02:11.000
so a reachability check passes it.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.370
What takes the core down is the size of the table.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:18.520
Each core router goes from a hundred and thirteen routes to more than nine thousand.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.037
So Hammerhead counts the routes landing on each device,

00:02:22.037 --> 00:02:24.408
compares that with the capacity you've declared,

00:02:24.408 --> 00:02:25.840
and fails the change on that.

00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:29.447
And it tells you what it couldn't read.

00:02:30.197 --> 00:02:31.706
This lab mixes four vendors.

00:02:31.706 --> 00:02:33.054
Hammerhead reads fifteen,

00:02:33.054 --> 00:02:37.097
but on this Cisco core it only understood forty-four percent of the config.

00:02:37.897 --> 00:02:40.658
It doesn't average that away into one comfortable number.

00:02:40.658 --> 00:02:42.498
It shows it per device, on the screen,

00:02:42.498 --> 00:02:44.097
so you know what to go and check.

00:02:44.847 --> 00:02:47.616
And if a change touches something it can't model,

00:02:47.616 --> 00:02:50.047
it won't call that a pass. It says unknown.

00:02:51.467 --> 00:02:52.967
And it checks its own work.

00:02:53.717 --> 00:02:55.158
When it finds a policy leak,

00:02:55.158 --> 00:02:56.084
it writes the fix,

00:02:56.084 --> 00:02:58.091
waits for a named person to approve it,

00:02:58.091 --> 00:02:59.738
and hands your Ansible a bundle.

00:02:59.738 --> 00:03:02.157
Then it checks whether the fix actually landed.

00:03:02.957 --> 00:03:05.816
If we give it a snapshot where the change never applied,

00:03:05.816 --> 00:03:07.297
it refuses to close the case.

00:03:08.197 --> 00:03:09.191
That's deliberate.

00:03:09.191 --> 00:03:10.903
If it said verified every time,

00:03:10.903 --> 00:03:12.837
you'd have no reason to believe it.

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:16.007
Thursday, one fifty-two.

00:03:16.807 --> 00:03:19.047
The change didn't ship, and nobody got paged.

00:03:20.147 --> 00:03:21.197
If you want to try it,

00:03:21.197 --> 00:03:22.439
pick a site you know well,

00:03:22.439 --> 00:03:24.301
and the change you're least sure about.

00:03:24.301 --> 00:03:25.447
And break it here first.
